      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dublin was named after Dublin, Ireland, by original postmaster Jonathan Sawyer, reflecting Irish settlers' nostalgia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dublin%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits on the Oconee River, which joins the Ocmulgee to form the Altamaha that empties into the Atlantic.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dublin%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1886 the Dublin & Wrightsville Railroad opened, sparking a boom that eventually linked Dublin to five major rail lines.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://courierheraldtoday.com/1887-the-year-dublin-was-reborn/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic Dublin Carnegie Library, built in 1904 with Andrew Carnegie funds, served the community until the 1960s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
